hi there steve kaufman here  today i want to talk about  the three stages in language acquisition  uh picking up on a theme in my blog  now at the recent  portland oregon um  conference that i attended  on story listening stephen craftsman  gave a talk  and explained what he saw as the three  stages of language acquisition  the first stage is storytelling or story  listening that's in a classroom where  the teacher engages the learners and by  being very animated and writing things  and drawing things on a whiteboard or  blackboard  meaning is imparted  students start to  pick up some meaning in a language that  they are  being exposed to for the first time or  at any rate are beginners in so the  first stage is sort of stories  animated by a very animated teacher  trying to connect the learner to the  language  the second stage  to krashen is the reading of fiction he  believes that fiction which is against  stories fiction  is inherently i guess in his view more  interesting than non-fiction  and that in that second stage we  should be reading and typically graded  readers graded readers of um  fiction  and that then would eventually take the  the the learner to a third stage which  is  sort of um  a stage where they can read whatever  they want and really get into the  language okay that's crashing to do my  view and i of course  can't claim to be as knowledgeable as  crash and i can only refer to my own  experience to me the three stages are  first stage the language is unfamiliar  uh you can't tell where  as i said before one word ends and the  next word begins it's just noise you  don't know what the functions of the  words are nothing  you have to get yourself to some level  of familiarity with the language some  sense of how the language works  word order for example which very often  in textbooks that go to great length to  explain and which in fact we figure out  pretty quickly  uh so how does the language work the  verb comes at the end at the beginning  adjectives precede or don't or come  after the noun all of these things we  start to pick up on we start to get a  few we learn a few words  the language stops being noise but still  it's very difficult to understand even  after having read something and looked  up the words and we listen again and we  read again and we listen again and it's  still very difficult we're struggling to  get  a sort of a toehold in the language and  this can go on for three months or so  okay or call it 100 hours or 150 hours  lots of reading and listening the  occasional flipping through a grammar  book  not really remembering any of the rules  not noticing really a lot of the things  that they say there about how the  language functions because again the  grammar is explaining something that we  have no experience with  so that's phase number one the second  stage  is where we now  we don't have to listen  you know 25 30 times to the same story  we get more meaning we understand a lot  of what we hear  we have accumulated more words  maybe we have a few thousand words in  the language i'm starting to get to that  level in my arabic  but if we listen to something we haven't  been able to read we still don't  understand it we have a lot of trouble  remembering words  but we plow forward and  dealing again sort of a mixture of  content that's designed for the learner  with more challenging stuff that we can  really only understand if we're like on  my ipad and i can look up words and so  forth and then listen to it and so  you know we're starting to become  somewhat  sort of confident  confident that we are going to overcome  the language but still far from being  able to function in the language and  that's that second stage can continue  for quite a while and maybe that's  another couple hundred hours it depends  on the language obviously arabic and  chinese are going to be more of a  challenge than say spanish  and now the third stage and there i kind  of rejoin krashen the third stage is is  where we now have so many words  that we can read articles in the  newspaper and there's only going to be  let's say less than 10 new words there  so we are able to enjoy the language  uh and continue forever improving in the  language  while  reading things of interest listening to  things of interest  we have now engaged in the language so  if we look at these stages and relate  them to the european sort of common  european  framework  so the first stage is a beginner medium  stage is intermediate and i would say  that even the the final stage starts  somewhere  while we are still intermediate you know  we haven't yet once we're at b2  we're we're swimming we're  able to sort of operate in the language  we're able to speak to people we have  vocabulary we have comprehension we're  missing things and we're now in a  position to continue forever to improve  ourselves in the language so those are  what i see as the three stages most  people never get past the first stage  they never get to the to where they're  confident that they're going to learn  the language where they get this  momentum behind them and they're  starting to consume more and more  uh  reading and listening  which will eventually take them to the  third stage most people get frustrated  by their inability to kind of get a  sense of the language and part of that i  think is because there's too much  pressure on them to produce the language  and so they are frustrated that they  they're sort of continuing mistakes in  the language and of course i can't say  anything in arabic i haven't really i'm  going to start speaking in arabic i know  that i'll have a lot of trouble but it  doesn't frustrate me and i think if we  can overcome this sense of frustration  or inadequacy or concern about making  mistakes then we can move out of that  first stage  into the second stage and then just  continue and work our way towards  fluency there you are three stages in  language learning thanks for listening  bye for now  [Music]  you
 

